<Article slug="soda-firing">
  <p>
    Most pottery is glazed the way a wall is painted: a coating is mixed, applied, and fired into
    place. Soda firing works differently. Part of the surface is made inside the kiln, by the
    atmosphere itself, at the moment the fire is hottest.
  </p>

  <blockquote>&ldquo;I make pots and then hand them to the fire.&rdquo;</blockquote>

  <h2>A glaze made of vapour</h2>
  <p>
    Near the top of the firing, at around cone 10 (roughly 2,350&deg;F, or 1,285&deg;C), soda ash
    or a similar sodium compound is introduced into the firebox, usually sprayed in as a solution
    or dropped in as a damp mix. At that heat it turns to vapour almost instantly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The flame carries the sodium vapour through the kiln. Wherever it lands, it reacts with the
    silica and alumina already in the clay, melting the surface into a thin, glassy skin. In other
    words, the clay becomes part of its own glaze.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the colour moves</h2>
  <p>
    Because the vapour travels with the flame, it does not settle evenly. The side of a pot that
    faced the burner may be glossy and richly coloured, while the side in shadow stays dry and
    pale. Iron and titanium in the clay and in thin slips react with the sodium to produce the
    warm oranges and rusts known as <em>flashing</em>. Where the soda runs thick it can pool and
    drip; where it barely reaches, the bare stoneware shows through.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why two mugs made the same day, glazed the same way and fired side by side, will
    still leave the kiln as two different objects. The rust on a Milzy piece is a record of where
    it sat and how the fire moved past it.
  </p>

  <h2>A descendant of salt glaze</h2>
  <p>
    Soda firing grew out of salt glazing, a technique used in Germany since the Middle Ages, in
    which common salt is thrown into the kiln. Salt gives beautiful results but releases harsh
    chlorine fumes. From the 1970s onward, studio potters began replacing salt with sodium
    carbonate and sodium bicarbonate, which give a related surface while being far kinder to the
    potter and the air.
  </p>

  <h2>Half me, half accident</h2>
  <p>
    Every decision before the firing is deliberate: the form, the facets, where glaze goes and
    where clay is left bare, where each pot sits in the kiln. After the soda goes in, the potter
    can only watch. The best pieces are the ones where those two halves meet: an intention, and a
    response from the fire that could not have been planned.
  </p>
</Article>
